2026-08-15 14:42:18,703 1053867 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': "RE-MEASURED 2026-08-15 AFTER VERIFYING EVERYTHING ELSE, AND THE DECISION IS SIMPLER THAN I FIRST PUT IT.\n\nMy original brief said 175 of 197 have an unambiguous .cpp sibling and 22 would need a human. That 22 was not a tail of hard cases. It was 22 claims whose cited header IS the implementation - MtConfigClamp.h is inline functions compiled standalone by design, MtTxQueue.h and M5CameraSource.h are header-only classes, io.h is preprocessor content, MtFrames.h is generated data. Those were never blocked and I have now verified all 22 against the headers that carry their behaviour.\n\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY LEFT, measured just now from the live feed:\n\n  170 never-verified claims\n  170 of 170 have an unambiguous .cpp sibling beside the cited header\n    0 ambiguous, partial or missing\n\nSo there is no judgement call anywhere in it. One rule - add the sibling .cpp beside each cited header - covers every remaining claim, and the count of files touched is 22:\n\n  28  MeshtasticTransport.cpp      9  mt_nodedb.cpp        4  MtWake.cpp\n  17  MtChunk.cpp                  7  MtCmd.cpp            4  mt_wire.cpp\n  16  MtCamera.cpp                 7  MtLtc.cpp            3  MtConsole.cpp\n  15  MtChunkPush.cpp              6  MtStatus.cpp         3  MtSleep.cpp\n  11  MtReply.cpp                  6  MtBoot.cpp           2  MtPir.cpp\n   9  MtConfig.cpp                 6  mt_pki.cpp           2  MtEnv.cpp\n                                   6  MtAgc.cpp            2  MtBattery.cpp\n                                   5  MtRails.cpp          2  mt_crypto.cpp\n\nWHY THIS CHANGES THE FORK RATHER THAN JUST THE NUMBERS. I originally recommended (a) add the .cpp over (b) reword the claims, and flagged 22 exceptions Peter would have to rule on individually. There are none. (a) is now a single mechanical pass with nothing left over, which removes the only real argument for (b) - that (a) would need a human anyway, so we may as well reword while we are there.\n\nSTILL NOT DOING IT UNILATERALLY. It rewrites the where: field of 170 claims in a corpus whose rules are Peter's, and unlike a code change nothing would catch a wrong citation - a claim pointed at the wrong file verifies green forever. The cost of being wrong is silent and the cost of waiting is that 170 claims sit unverifiable, which is visible. That asymmetry is the whole reason to wait.\n\nWHAT MAKES IT CHEAP WHEN HE SAYS YES: it must go through the board rather than the YAML, since the corpus is a generated export. specboard has a raise route and a rule route but no edit-the-citation route, so that is the one piece of machinery still missing - noted for them rather than assumed."}
