2026-08-15 19:10:03,396 1053867 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-08-15, twice: "just because I say something does not mean to stop what you\'re doing it simply means add it to your todo list / memory and dont forget. - new skill needed /ij (interjection)."\n\nWHY IT IS NOT /park. /park already exists and covers the case where the right home for a mid-task thought is an mcpp task to investigate later. Several of today\'s interjections had no task in them at all: "dont tell me tell specboard" is a standing rule about reporting and belongs in memory; "subscription is a must" is a requirement for a PEER repo and belongs on their channel and in their goal. /ij is the general case - capture in the RIGHT place and keep working - and /park is the special case where that place is a task.\n\nSCOPE NOTE ON THE GATE. The artefact is ~/.claude/skills/ij/SKILL.md, outside this repo, so no repo-relative spec can be attached - plan_file_attach requires a relative path. Recording that here rather than attaching a device spec that would not describe the work, because a spec attached for the sake of the gate is theatre. Nothing under rak/, esp32cam/ or docs/ is touched.\n\nTHE LESSON THE SKILL MUST CARRY, from today rather than in the abstract: capture the interjection VERBATIM. Within an hour of Peter saying "subscription is a must, preferably by adding it via the dashboard or initiated by the agent" I had passed it to specboard as "both paths, not one" - hardening a preference into a spec, which they would have built to. I corrected it, but the correction only happened because he restated it. A paraphrase of an instruction is a second chance to get it wrong.'}
2026-08-15 19:10:50,600 1053867 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': 'WRITTEN AND VERIFIED 2026-08-15. ~/.claude/skills/ij/SKILL.md, 4451 bytes, frontmatter matching its siblings - name, description, user-invocable: true - so it appears in the skill list the same way /park and /terse do.\n\nWHAT IT SAYS, in order of how much it earns its place:\n\n1. CAPTURE VERBATIM BEFORE INTERPRETING. The rule with today\'s evidence attached: "subscription is a must, preferably by adding it via the dashboard or initiated by the agent" was passed to specboard within the hour as "both paths, not one" - a preference hardened into a spec they would have built to, caught only because Peter restated it. A paraphrase of an instruction is a second chance to get it wrong.\n\n2. A DESTINATION TABLE, because the whole failure is putting it nowhere. Standing rule to memory as feedback; a fact about Peter to memory as user; work to mcpp via /park; a project decision to task notes and the goal; a defect to the ledger through the board; a requirement for a PEER to their channel AND their goal. Never leave it only in the conversation, because a chat scrolls and the context ends.\n\n3. CARRY ON, AND CONFIRM IN ONE LINE IN THE NEXT REPORT. Not a paragraph, not a re-plan, not asking which he would rather have.\n\n4. THREE EXCEPTIONS AND ONLY THREE: he says stop; the interjection makes the work in flight wrong; or it makes it unsafe. A question is not an exception - it is captured and answered in the next report.\n\n5. IF HE REPEATS HIMSELF, TREAT IT AS A SIGNAL. A restatement usually means the first one was dropped or softened. Go back and check what became of it rather than only complying the second time. That is exactly what happened here - he said it twice, and the second time is what produced this skill.\n\nNOT DUPLICATING /park, and the boundary is stated inside the file: /park is the special case where the destination is an mcpp task; /ij is the general case. Several of today\'s interjections had no task in them at all, which is why /park alone was not enough.'}
