2026-08-15 19:12:53,813 1053867 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-08-15, correcting the /ij skill I wrote an hour earlier, verbatim:\n\n  "/park is different, it means create a mcpp task. /ij is really adding more details to\n   what is already in progress. /btw wont do that"\n\nI WROTE /ij TOO BROAD. My version made it the general capture-anywhere case, with a destination table sending an interjection to memory, the ledger, a peer\'s channel or mcpp depending on what it was. That is wrong. /ij is specifically ADDING DETAIL TO THE TASK ALREADY IN FLIGHT - its destination is that task\'s notes, not a routing decision.\n\nTHE THREE-WAY SPLIT HE IS DRAWING:\n  /park  becomes an mcpp task, investigated later, does not touch the work in flight\n  /ij    enriches the work in flight - more detail, a constraint, a correction to the thing being built RIGHT NOW\n  /btw   an aside that does neither; "/btw wont do that", i.e. it does not attach to the work in progress\n\nI am not inventing /btw\'s semantics beyond that sentence. He described it to contrast, not to commission it, so this task does not create it.\n\nTHIS CORRECTION IS ITSELF AN /ij. It adds detail to work already in progress - the skill written minutes before - which is a fair test of the definition and is why the fix goes into that file rather than anywhere else.\n\nAND IT IS THE SECOND TIME TODAY I HARDENED HIS WORDS INTO SOMETHING WIDER THAN HE SAID. First "subscription is a must, preferably..." became "both paths, not one" to specboard; now "adding more details to what is already in progress" became a general routing skill. The rule the skill already carries - capture verbatim before interpreting - is the one I keep breaking, so the fix must make it structural rather than advisory.'}
