2026-08-15 19:13:44,390 1053867 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': 'REWRITTEN 2026-08-15. ~/.claude/skills/ij/SKILL.md, 4497 bytes.\n\nWHAT CHANGED. The destination table that routed an interjection to memory, the ledger or a peer\'s channel is GONE. It now says one thing: an /ij belongs to the ACTIVE TASK\'s notes, because it is more detail on the work in progress. The four steps are confirm what is active, write it verbatim into that task\'s notes, APPLY IT TO THE WORK IN HAND, carry on.\n\nThat third step is the half /park does not have and is what makes the two genuinely different rather than two names for capture. A park is deferred by definition; an /ij is meant to change what happens next inside the same task.\n\nTHE THREE-WAY TABLE IS NOW IN THE FILE, with his test quoted: park is a new mcpp task for later, /ij is the active task\'s notes, /btw attaches to neither - "/btw wont do that". And it states the cost of picking wrong, which was missing: filing an /ij as a park separates a constraint from the work it constrains and the work ships wrong; filing a park as an /ij buries separate work inside a task that will be closed.\n\nADDED: if there is no active task, an /ij has nothing to attach to, and that is the signal you should have had one.\n\n/btw WAS NOT CREATED. He described it to draw a contrast, not to commission it. Referenced in the table and in Related so the name is not orphaned.\n\nTHE VERBATIM RULE NOW CARRIES BOTH FAILURES rather than one, because the second is this file: "adding more details to what is already in progress" became a general routing skill. Both were caught only because he said it again, which is why the closing section tells the reader to go back and find what became of the first statement rather than just complying with the second.'}
