2026-08-16 10:43:11,663 1053867 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'mark-survey', 'title': 'MEASURE what reached the mesh from Milverton — pings and traceroutes from TA2m at Butts Way', 'steps': ['MEASURE gather every packet heard from TA2m and which radio heard it', 'MEASURE the traceroutes and what path they report', 'AGREE a summary of what the site can and cannot reach']}
2026-08-16 10:46:37,625 1053867 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-08-16: "I just sent probably a dozen pings from TA2m and some traceroutes as well from a different remote site in Milverton: The GPS coordinates for Butts Way in Milverton, Taunton, Somerset are approximately 51.023 N, 3.261 W (exact latitude/longitude: 51.023089, -3.260549). gather all the evidence you can find and summarise it."\n\nTHE SESSION IS 2026-08-16, 10:00:14 to 10:34:53 BST. Eleven distinct packets from TA2m (!da5af428, node 3663393832, "B12PAC car", TLORA_T3_S3 - a vehicle-mounted node). Every one was heard, and every one was heard by BOTH gateway radios: 22 receptions, 11 packets, zero misses. "Probably a dozen" is eleven.\n\nRADIO CONFIGURATION, read from the gateway rather than assumed: SF11, BW250, CR5, EU_868, hop_limit 7, tx_power 27, rx_boosted_gain on. That is Meshtastic LongFast, and the SF11 demodulation floor is about -17.5 dB SNR - the number that makes the rest of this readable.\n\nTHE LABELS ARE THE EXPERIMENT. Four unlabelled pings, then orange container, small container, drum, car stick, car stubby, hillcommon. Antenna and siting variations, one per ping.\n\nWHAT COULD NOT BE FOUND, and it is not for want of looking: THE TRACEROUTES ARE NOT RECOVERABLE HERE. mesh-gw exposes POST /{node_id}/traceroute, which INITIATES one, and there is no GET and no store - I read its own openapi.json rather than guessing. The bridge recorder has zero rows matching traceroute and zero rows mentioning TA2m at all; it records the alarm units only. There is no tap log running (/tmp/meshgw-tap.jsonl absent). So the traceroute results exist on the handheld and nowhere on this host. If they matter, they have to come off the phone.\n\nALSO NOT AVAILABLE: position. TA2m\'s node record carries a position object with a timestamp and no coordinates, and not one of the 516 nodes in the gateway\'s table has lat/lon. So the Milverton location is Peter\'s word and the coordinates he supplied; nothing on the mesh corroborates it and no distance can be computed from mesh data.'}
2026-08-16 10:46:55,624 1053867 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': 'THE TRAP IN THIS DATA, AND IT INVERTS THE OBVIOUS READING. rssi and snr describe the LAST HOP ONLY - the link from whatever transmitted the copy the gateway heard. On a relayed packet that is the relay\'s signal, not Milverton\'s. So the two best-looking rows in the table are the two that say least about the site:\n\n  hillcommon   OMNI -93 / +5.75 / 1 hop     the relay was close, not Milverton\n  car stubby   OMNI -113 / -2.5 / 2 hops    two relays away; tells you nothing about the car\n\nComparing "car stick" (direct, -127/-17.0) against "car stubby" (relayed, -113/-2.5) and concluding the stubby is the better antenna would be exactly backwards. They are measurements of different links.\n\nTHE DIRECT PATH ONLY, hops=0, which is the real Milverton-to-gateway measurement:\n\n  small container    YAGI -114 / -4.75      best labelled direct\n  small container    OMNI -116 / -5.5\n  orange container   OMNI -116 / -5.75\n  orange container   YAGI -118 / -7.0\n  car stick          YAGI -124 / -13.0\n  car stick          OMNI -127 / -17.0      at the SF11 floor\n  drum               YAGI -128 / -20.5      past the SF11 floor\n  (unlabelled early) both  -117 to -121 / -5.75 to -10.0\n\nTHE FINDING. Milverton reaches the mesh on every attempt, but the DIRECT path is marginal to the point of being unreliable: most direct receptions sit between -5 and -10 dB SNR, and three are at or past the -17.5 dB SF11 demodulation floor. Getting a packet through at -20.5 dB is luck, not margin. What made the session look flawless is the MESH, not the link: eleven for eleven arrived because relays carried the ones the direct path could not.\n\nRANKING THE ANTENNAS, with the caveat that each label has one or two samples and the difference between the best and worst direct pair is about 8 dB:\n  1. small container   -4.75 / -5.5    clearly the best of the labelled positions\n  2. orange container  -5.75 / -7.0    close behind\n  3. car stick         -13.0 / -17.0   markedly worse, at the edge\n  4. drum              -20.5           one direct sample, past the floor\n  car stubby and hillcommon produced NO direct sample at all, so they are unranked - not bad, unmeasured.\n\nWHAT WOULD SETTLE IT: repeat the set from one fixed position with several pings per configuration, and record whether each arrived direct. One sample per antenna at the edge of demodulation cannot separate an antenna difference from ordinary fading - the spread within the unlabelled pings alone is 4 dB.'}
2026-08-16 10:50:02,045 1053867 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-16, reframing the survey mid-analysis, verbatim:\n\n  "well a direct is great, but reliability via the mesh is more important"\n\nHe is right and my summary led with the wrong number. I reported "the direct path is marginal" as though that were the result. It is not the result; it is a property of one route. The result is that ELEVEN OF ELEVEN ARRIVED.\n\nRE-ANALYSED AGAINST HIS CRITERION - what would have happened WITHOUT relays:\n\n  no direct copy at all      3 of 11    the mesh was the only route\n  direct only at/past floor  2 of 11    direct would be a coin toss\n  direct with real margin    6 of 11\n\n  10:00:39  (unlabelled)      0 direct, 2 relayed   LOST without the mesh\n  10:26:36  car stubby        0 direct, 2 relayed   LOST without the mesh\n  10:34:31  hillcommon        0 direct, 2 relayed   LOST without the mesh\n  10:00:14  Ping              direct at -17.75 dB   at/past the SF11 floor\n  10:17:05  drum              direct at -20.50 dB   past the floor\n\nSo a direct-only link from Butts Way delivers 6 of 11 with confidence and 8 of 11 at best. THE MESH DELIVERED 11 OF 11. That is the headline and I buried it under an antenna ranking.\n\nAND IT REVERSES THE ANTENNA CONCLUSION TOO. I ranked car stubby and hillcommon as "unranked - no direct sample". Under Peter\'s criterion that is not a gap in the data, it IS the data: both configurations reached the mesh with no direct path available at all, which is the harder thing to do and the thing that matters. Ranking by direct SNR ranks the wrong axis.\n\nWHAT THE SURVEY SHOULD MEASURE, restated: not how strong the direct link is, but how many independent ways a packet can arrive. On that measure Butts Way looks good - every packet arrived, three of them by a route that did not exist directly.\n\nONE WEAKNESS IN THE EVIDENCE, stated because it bounds the claim: the two gateway radios heard the same packet by DIFFERENT hop counts on only 2 of 11 packets. On the other nine both radios saw the same path shape, so they are not nine independent confirmations - the redundancy measured here is mostly one path heard twice, not two paths. Establishing real path diversity needs the relay identity per reception, which the message feed does not carry; the witness records do carry a relay field but they only cover the alarm units, not TA2m.'}
2026-08-16 10:50:38,254 1053867 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-16, verbatim:\n\n  "but the point is it looks like we received every ping I did. next time I will number them"\n\nHE HAS CAUGHT A TAUTOLOGY IN MY OWN REPORTING AND HE IS RIGHT. I wrote "11 of 11, zero misses". That is not a measurement. The feed only contains packets that ARRIVED, so the denominator is the numerator by construction - I counted eleven arrivals and reported eleven of eleven. A ping that never made it leaves no trace anywhere on this host: no gap, no gap marker, nothing. "Zero misses among the ones that were not missed" is what I actually established.\n\nAND THE EVIDENCE THAT IT MATTERS IS ALREADY IN HIS OWN WORDS. He said "probably a dozen". I found eleven. That difference is either his estimate being loose or one ping lost, and NOTHING IN THE DATA CAN TELL THOSE APART. It is exactly the size of gap that would go unnoticed.\n\nWHY NUMBERING FIXES IT, and it fixes more than the count:\n  - a missing number is a loss, positively identified, with no reliance on the sender remembering how many were sent\n  - it ties a loss to a CONFIGURATION - lose number 7 and you know which antenna was on the car\n  - it gives a real denominator, so "delivery rate" becomes a rate rather than a restatement\n  - a duplicate number would expose a rebroadcast being counted as a fresh arrival, which nothing here currently distinguishes either\n\nSO THE HONEST STATEMENT OF THIS SURVEY\'S RESULT: eleven packets arrived, every one heard by both radios, three of them only because the mesh relayed them. WHETHER ANY WERE LOST IS UNKNOWN AND UNKNOWABLE FROM THIS END. The mesh-reliability conclusion stands on what got through; it does not have a loss rate behind it and I should not have implied one.\n\nFOR THE NEXT RUN: numbered pings, several per configuration, from a fixed position. That turns this from a set of anecdotes into a measurement.'}
2026-08-16 10:54:56,718 1053867 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-16, verbatim: "yagi is swr 1.2 at 868mhz"\n\nWHAT THAT SETTLES AND WHAT IT DOES NOT.\n\nSWR 1.2:1 is a return loss of about 20.8 dB - roughly 0.8% of the power reflected. The antenna is properly matched at the operating frequency, so the first of my three explanations is DEAD: this is not a bad feed, a bad match, or an antenna mistuned for 868.\n\nBut SWR measures how well power gets INTO the antenna, not where it goes afterwards. A dummy load has a perfect SWR and no pattern at all. So 1.2 does not establish gain and does not establish front-to-back ratio. What it does do is make "a correctly built yagi" the likely reading - and a correctly built 868 MHz yagi should have a front-to-back ratio of 15-20 dB.\n\nWHICH MAKES THE ANOMALY SHARPER RATHER THAN SMALLER. A well-matched yagi, 144 degrees off its boresight, matched an omni across six direct packets - mean +0.25 dB, and it BEAT the omni on two of them. If the pattern is what a matched yagi\'s pattern normally is, that cannot happen for a signal arriving from 265 degrees.\n\nSO THE REMAINING EXPLANATION IS THE INTERESTING ONE: THE SIGNAL IS NOT ARRIVING FROM 265 DEGREES. At 7.5 km across Somerset the path is very unlikely to be line of sight; the energy is diffracting or scattering off terrain and arriving from whatever direction the ground allows. The geometric bearing is where Milverton IS, not where the signal COMES FROM.\n\nAND THAT REVERSES THE PREDICTION I gave an hour ago. I said pointing the yagi at Milverton would probably help and that "worse" was a risk worth stating. With the match ruled out, worse is now the more likely outcome of aiming at 265: you would be swinging the main lobe away from wherever the energy actually is.\n\nTHE EXPERIMENT CHANGES SHAPE TOO. A single aim at 265 tests the wrong hypothesis. What is worth doing is a SWEEP - park the rotator at a series of azimuths with numbered pings from a fixed spot at each, and find where the peak actually is. That measures the path rather than assuming it, and the answer is useful for siting anything else out that way.\n\nCONFLICT TO PLAN AROUND: the rotator\'s day job is aiming at GARG, and pointer.aim re-fires from geometry every 15 minutes, so a survey aim has to be held against that.'}
2026-08-16 10:57:31,952 1053867 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-16, verbatim: "I never said that garage would be the only remote"\n\nCORRECTING AN ASSUMPTION I HAD BAKED INTO THE ANALYSIS. I described the rotator\'s aim at GARG as its "day job" and the survey as an interruption of it. That only holds while there is one remote site. Milverton may be a candidate DEPLOYMENT, not a curiosity, and that changes what the survey is for.\n\nTWO CONSEQUENCES, one geometric and one in this repo\'s firmware.\n\nGEOMETRY. GARG sits at 121 degrees and Milverton at 265 - 144 degrees apart. One rotator with one yagi cannot serve both. Time-sharing leaves each site unaimed most of the time, and the aim only holds 16 seconds per pointer.aim anyway. Three or more remotes makes the directional antenna the wrong instrument entirely.\n\nFIRMWARE, AND THIS IS THE BLOCKER. main.cpp:2890 is:\n\n    g_roleIdx = g_usbAtBoot ? SITE_BENCH : SITE_GARAGE;\n\nThe site model is BINARY. Cable in is bench, no cable is garage, and there is no third value and no way to express one. Deploy a second unit at Milverton today and it boots claiming to be GARG - same short and long names, same coordinates, same beat and sleep defaults - and broadcasts the garage\'s position. Two units on the mesh each insisting they are the same site.\n\nNothing in the corpus records this. The claims describe the two-site behaviour as correct, because two sites is all there has ever been, so a verification pass reads it as working. It is working; it is just not what a second remote needs.\n\nWHAT TODAY\'S DATA SAYS ABOUT A SECOND SITE, which is the question that actually matters now: the mesh is what would make Milverton viable, not the pointing hardware. Off-boresight the yagi matched the omni, and three of eleven packets arrived ONLY because something relayed them. Aiming cannot serve two sites at once regardless of how well it works.\n\nTWO THINGS THAT NEED PETER: whether the firmware should be able to express a third site at all - that is real work, since the cable is currently the only signal a unit has about which site it is - and how many remotes he has in mind, because two is a rotator scheduling problem and three makes it an omni-and-mesh design.'}
2026-08-16 10:57:38,906 1053867 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_file_attach args={'file_path': 'docs/PROJECT-GOAL.md', 'note': "Governing spec: Peter's terseness rule applies to this artefact more than most - a reference he cannot scan is not a reference. SCOPE FOR THIS TASK IS ONE NEW FILE: docs/surveys/2026-08-16-milverton.md. Nothing under rak/ or esp32cam/ is in scope; this repo is read-only on code until Peter lifts it, and the survey findings that touch firmware (the binary site model at main.cpp:2890) are RECORDED, not fixed."}
2026-08-16 11:07:06,441 1053867 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-16, verbatim: "AFAIK, there is no physical limit on number of remotes, and all that is needed is for each to have it\'s coords correct and a suitable short / long name"\n\nHE IS SUBSTANTIALLY RIGHT AND I OVERSTATED THE BLOCKER. I wrote "the firmware cannot express a second site". That is wrong. SITE_ROLES is an ARRAY and every per-site fact already lives in it - shortName, longName, lat, lon, capacityMah, heartbeatMsDefault, sleepDefault, role. Adding a third or tenth row is pure data. There is no structural limit and no code change needed for the table itself.\n\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY BINARY IS THE SELECTOR, NOT THE TABLE. main.cpp:2890 is one bit:\n\n    g_roleIdx = g_usbAtBoot ? SITE_BENCH : SITE_GARAGE;\n\nOne bit of identity - VBUS at boot - chooses between two rows. Add a third row and nothing can select it. So the constraint is not "N sites cannot be described", it is "a unit has only one bit with which to say which one it is".\n\nAND THAT IS SMALLER THAN I MADE IT SOUND, because the record already knows. MtConfig persists roleIdx and already detects a site change in order to re-baseline beat and sleep. The stored site exists; the boot-time selection simply does not consult it.\n\nTHE REASON IS ORDERING, AND IT IS DOCUMENTED AND MEASURED. main.cpp:2881-2884: "IT NEEDS NO FILESYSTEM, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS LEGAL HERE. InternalFS.begin() is ~80 lines below. f58b152 tried to select the site from a PERSISTED record at this point and called MtConfig::peekRoleIdx(), which opens SETTINGS_PATH before the mount -- so the stored site could never be honoured." Verified: selection is at :2890, InternalFS.begin() is at :2963. Seventy-three lines.\n\nSo the persisted-site design was tried, failed for a mount-ordering reason, and was reverted - not abandoned on merit.\n\nTHIS GROUND HAS BEEN WALKED TWICE MORE. :2871-2878 records -DSITE_GARAGE (identity in the binary) and then FICR->DEVICEID with a table of serials. The SERIAL TABLE WAS the N-site mechanism, and it was deleted 2026-08-12 "because all boards are now identical and a serial is not what distinguishes them". True of two identical boards; not true once there are several sites that need telling apart.\n\nWHAT WOULD ACTUALLY BE NEEDED, and it is a design question for Peter rather than a blocker: something other than the cable to carry site identity. Either select provisionally from VBUS and RE-SELECT after the mount from the persisted roleIdx, or set the site by command once and let the record hold it. The re-select has a hazard the file already names at :2884-2885 - g_role is read into names, coordinates and defaults, and "a live role makes all of those change underneath everything that already cached them" - so a late re-select has to land before anything caches. The mount at :2963 is before loadSettings, so the window looks available; I have not proved it.\n\nCORRECTING THE SURVEY DOC, since it carries the overstated version and is meant to be a reference.'}
2026-08-16 11:11:50,844 1053867 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-16, verbatim: "so there\'s something you can do right now. I gave you the coords, from that you can determine the elevation and add to secrets."\n\nTHE ELEVATION, WHICH IS THE PART I CAN DELIVER. Butts Way 51.023089, -3.260549:\n\n    EU-DEM 25m   100.7 m\n    SRTM  30m    101.0 m       -> 101 m, two independent DEMs agreeing to 0.3 m\n\nFOR CONTEXT, same two sources, same call:\n    BENCH/HOME   28.0 / 30.0 m\n    GARAGE       25.6 / 26.0 m\n    MILVERTON   100.7 / 101.0 m\n\nMILVERTON IS 73 M ABOVE HOME. That is not a detail - it is most of why a 7.5 km path works at all.\n\nI CANNOT ADD IT TO SECRETS, AND THERE ARE THREE SEPARATE REASONS, ANY ONE OF WHICH STOPS IT.\n\nONE - THE FILE IS NOT IN THIS REPO. rak/include/secrets.h does not exist here. It is gitignored (.gitignore:4) and the only copies on disk are in the OLD tree, /usr/share/pac/dev/projects/mt-alarm/device{,.0,.1}/. So this repo cannot build the firmware as it stands, and there is nothing here to edit. Everything I know about SiteRole below was read out of the old tree.\n\nTWO - THERE IS NO ALTITUDE FIELD. struct SiteRole is role, longName, shortName, lat, lon, capacityMah, sleepDefault, heartbeatMsDefault. Two doubles for position and nothing vertical. Adding elevation is a STRUCT change, not a data addition - which is the one place Peter\'s "all that is needed is coords and names" does not hold. And grep for altitude across main.cpp and every lib returns NOTHING, so no consumer exists either: the firmware never puts an altitude in a position. Adding the field changes nothing on air until something reads it.\n\nTHREE - AND THIS IS THE ONE I UNDER-CALLED EARLIER. secrets.h ends:\n\n    static_assert(SITE_ROLES_COUNT == 2,\n        "SITE_ROLES is exactly bench and garage. USB decides which; there is no third state.");\n\nA THIRD ROW DOES NOT FAIL TO BE SELECTABLE. IT FAILS TO COMPILE. I told Peter the selector was the constraint and the table was fine. The table is not fine - it carries an explicit assertion that there are exactly two rows, and the comment above it says "ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING AGAIN... roleIdx is persisted, so inserting or swapping a row changes what a stored record means". So a Milverton row is a build break plus a persisted-record migration, not data entry. Correcting that in the survey doc too.\n\nALSO: I am READONLY on code by Peter\'s own standing instruction, and no spec is attached for a secrets change. Even without the three above I would not make this edit unprompted.\n\nWHAT I DID INSTEAD, BECAUSE THE COORDS BUY MORE THAN ONE NUMBER - see the next note. The terrain profile answers the yagi question that the SWR figure left open.'}
2026-08-16 11:12:10,008 1053867 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': 'THE TERRAIN PROFILE, AND IT ANSWERS THE YAGI QUESTION THE SWR FIGURE LEFT OPEN.\n\nSampled EU-DEM 25m at 40 points along HOME -> MILVERTON, 7.53 km, and applied a k=4/3 earth bulge and the first Fresnel radius at 868 MHz.\n\nTHE PATH IS GEOMETRICALLY LINE OF SIGHT. Milverton at 101 m looks down on home at 28 m, and no ground point rises above the straight line between them. On a bare ground-vs-line test the path is clear end to end.\n\nBUT IT IS FRESNEL-OBSTRUCTED IN SIX PLACES. Assuming a 10 m home mast and a 2 m car antenna - BOTH ASSUMED, neither measured, and the answer moves with them:\n\n    0.56 km   45% of F1\n    1.88 km   56%\n    2.07 km   53%\n    3.58 km   52%\n    3.76 km   55%\n    6.21 km   36%   <- worst, ground 84.0 m, clearance +6.9 m against an F1 of 19.4 m\n\nBelow 60% of the first Fresnel zone the path stops behaving like free space and starts behaving like a diffraction problem. Six pinches, one at barely a third.\n\nWHY THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE ELEVATION ITSELF: IT SUPPLIES THE MECHANISM I WAS MISSING. I argued from the SWR figure that the signal probably is not arriving from 265 degrees, but I had no reason WHY beyond "Somerset". Now there is one. A path that clears the ground but not the Fresnel zone at six points is one where the arriving energy is the sum of several diffracted contributions over several ridges - and the direction that sum arrives from is set by the ridge geometry, not by the great-circle bearing. A yagi 144 degrees off the geometric bearing matching an omni is exactly what that produces.\n\nAND IT CHANGES THE RECOMMENDATION AGAIN, IN A USEFUL DIRECTION. The pinch at 6.21 km is near the far end and nothing here can fix it. But three of the six are in the first 4 km, near the HOME antenna, and those are the ones height fixes: Fresnel radius is small near the ends, so a few metres of mast buys back a large percentage there. RAISING THE HOME ANTENNA IS LIKELY WORTH MORE THAN AIMING IT, and it helps every direction at once rather than trading GARG for Milverton.\n\nBOUNDS ON THIS, STATED SO IT IS NOT OVER-READ:\n  - antenna heights are assumed, not measured. Give me the real home mast height and the car antenna height and I will re-run it; the percentages move materially.\n  - EU-DEM is a BARE EARTH model. Trees and buildings are not in it, so the real clearances are worse than these, never better. Somerset hedgerows and treelines at 15-20 m are exactly the scale of the numbers above.\n  - the sweep experiment is still the right measurement. This predicts where the peak is NOT; only a sweep finds where it is.'}
2026-08-16 11:17:30,031 1053867 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-16, verbatim: "rak/include/secrets.h is not in this repo - well it has to be doesnt it ?"\n\nYES, AND HE HAS PULLED A BIGGER THREAD THAN THE ONE HE ASKED ABOUT. I reported the missing secrets.h as a footnote to the elevation question. It is not a footnote. THIS REPO CANNOT BUILD AT ALL, for TWO independent reasons, and neither is recorded anywhere.\n\nI RAN THE BUILD RATHER THAN ASSERTING IT. `pio run -e v3` from rak/v3:\n\n    audit: port-write / dead-ifdef / spec-drift / dead-env   all ok\n    bump_fw: FW_VERSION = 3-260816-1?\n    FileNotFoundError: .../mt-alarm-1/device/schema/frames.json\n    schema/frames.json changed but MtSchema.h was not regenerated\n    [FAILED] Took 0.53 seconds\n\nBREAKAGE ONE - schema/frames.json WAS DELETED BY THE SPLIT COMMIT ITSELF. 924bb4c, "device becomes firmware only", 2026-08-14, two commits back, removed schema/frames.json (225 lines) IN THE SAME COMMIT that edited rak/v3/tools/gen_schema.py (6 lines). It deleted the file while touching the tool that reads it. It is NOT gitignored - it is a tracked file that was deliberately removed - and four live tools still require it:\n\n    rak/tools/gen_frames.py        rak/tools/check_frames.py\n    rak/v3/tools/gen_schema.py     rak/v3/tools/check_api_md.py\n\nand platformio.ini wires two of them as PRE-BUILD gates (:134 pre:../tools/check_frames.py, :138 pre:tools/gen_schema.py). So the build dies before it compiles a single line.\n\nFULLY RECOVERABLE FROM THIS REPO\'S OWN HISTORY: git show 924bb4c^:schema/frames.json - 225 lines, intact, verified readable.\n\nBREAKAGE TWO - secrets.h, AND THE DOCUMENTED FIX DOES NOT WORK. main.cpp:48 is #include "secrets.h". The file is gitignored (.gitignore:4) by design, so it is correctly absent from git and has to be created locally. platformio.ini:18 says how: "Copy ../include/secrets.h.example to ../include/secrets.h first".\n\nTHAT INSTRUCTION IS DEAD. The example defines MeshUnitConfig and MESH_UNITS. main.cpp needs SiteRole, SITE_ROLES, SITE_BENCH and SITE_GARAGE. grep -c SITE_ROLES on the example returns ZERO. The example was never updated through the 2026-08-12 site-model rewrite, so following the repo\'s own instructions produces a file that still does not compile.\n\nWHY NOTHING CAUGHT EITHER. The four audits at the top of the build output all PASS - port-write, dead-ifdef, spec-drift, dead-env. They run before the schema gate and none of them asks "can this tree build". A repo that reports four green ratchets and cannot compile is exactly the failure the ratchets were meant to prevent, and 924bb4c\'s own subject line is "the ratchets tell the truth".\n\nALSO RECONFIRMED LIVE: the \'?\' version suffix. bump_fw printed FW_VERSION = 3-260816-1? today. Same defect, still open.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT DO AND WHY. Both fixes are one command each and I have the material for both. I did neither: Peter\'s standing instruction is "in terms of code you are READONLY until I say otherwise", CLAUDE.md forbids shell redirects that bypass the Edit/Write gate, and no spec is attached for a build-input restore. The restore of a gitignored secrets file in particular is his call, not mine.\n\nRECOMMENDED, IN ORDER:\n  1. restore the schema from history - it is tracked, it was deleted by accident-looking intent, and the history has it\n  2. copy the real secrets.h across from the old tree (stays out of git, being ignored)\n  3. REFRESH secrets.h.example to the current site model, because the stale one is a trap for the next person who follows platformio.ini:18\n  4. add a build to whatever runs the ratchets, because four green audits over a repo that cannot compile is worse than no audits'}
2026-08-16 11:20:22,496 1053867 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-16, verbatim: "chema/frames.json is a share document, should be in the parent of device and bridge. mt-alarm" and, mid-turn: "both use it"\n\nHE IS RIGHT, IT IS ALREADY THERE, AND MY RECOMMENDATION WAS WRONG IN A WAY THAT WOULD HAVE CAUSED REAL HARM.\n\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY ON DISK. /usr/share/pac/dev/projects/mt-alarm-1/ is a git repo containing bridge/, device/, plugins/ and schema/. schema/frames.json is there, 17448 bytes, tracked in the OUTER repo at commit 5805155 whose subject is "the wire contract, owned by neither component". Diffed against the copy 924bb4c deleted from device: BYTE-IDENTICAL. NOTHING WAS LOST. IT MOVED, and it moved to exactly where Peter says it belongs.\n\nBOTH DO USE IT, AND BRIDGE ALREADY POINTS AT THE RIGHT PLACE. bridge/src/frames.js:22 resolves dirname/../../schema/frames.json = mt-alarm-1/schema/frames.json, correct. Its own header at :1 says "One function, driven by schema/frames.json - the same file the firmware\'s header" is generated from.\n\nMY ERROR, STATED PLAINLY. I told Peter to "restore the schema from history" into device. That would have created a SECOND copy of a file whose entire purpose is that there is only one, and it would have drifted from bridge\'s silently. gen_frames.py\'s own docstring names the failure: "The butler imports frames.json directly and cannot hold a stale copy." I recommended manufacturing exactly that. I had not acted only because he has me READONLY on code.\n\nTHE REAL DEFECT IS NARROWER AND IS IN MY REPO: TWO DEVICE TOOLS RESOLVE ONE LEVEL TOO SHALLOW, and they land on device/schema/ which is correctly empty.\n\n  rak/tools/gen_frames.py:20        HERE.parent.parent / \'schema\'      -> device/schema      BROKEN\n                                    needs .parent.parent.parent        -> mt-alarm-1/schema\n  rak/v3/tools/check_api_md.py:77   MAIN.parent x4 / \'schema\'          -> device/schema      BROKEN\n                                    needs .parent x5                   -> mt-alarm-1/schema\n\n  rak/tools/check_frames.py         delegates to gen_frames.py --check; fixed by fixing that one\n  rak/v3/tools/gen_schema.py:37     ALREADY CORRECT - do not touch\n\nAND I NEARLY GOT gen_schema.py WRONG TOO. My first pass tested it with ROOT = rak/v3/tools and called it broken. Its actual ROOT is rak/v3 - from $PROJECT_DIR under SCons, or dirname(dirname(__file__)) standalone - so \'..\',\'..\',\'..\' lands on mt-alarm-1/schema and it is right. Its comment already states the whole design: "THE SSOT, and it belongs to NEITHER repo. The firmware compiles against it and the bridge decodes with it, so it sits above both at <product-root>/schema/ rather than inside either. ROOT is device/rak/v3, so three levels up is the product root." Peter\'s correction is written in the source; two tools simply were not updated to match when bridge left device.\n\nSo 924bb4c did the right thing removing schema/ from device and updating gen_schema.py. What it missed is the other two path expressions. That is a one-line fix each, in this repo, and the build failure I saw is precisely gen_frames.py:154 failing to open device/schema/frames.json.\n\nTHE secrets.h FINDING IS UNCHANGED: still absent, still correctly gitignored, and secrets.h.example is still stale (MESH_UNITS/MeshUnitConfig where main.cpp needs SITE_ROLES/SiteRole), so platformio.ini:18\'s instruction still does not produce a buildable file.\n\nLESSON FOR MY OWN REPORTING: I asserted a file was missing without asking where it had gone. "Not in this repo" was true and useless; "moved to the shared parent, and two tools did not follow" is the finding. Peter had to supply the layout because I never looked one directory up.'}
